      "latency_ms": 24805.0,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 434,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is a compound made of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. This means every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** (Water in rivers, oceans, and rain)\n*   **Solid:** (Ice in glaciers, snow, and hail)\n*   **Gas:** (Water vapor, steam, and humidity in the air)\n\nAt standard temperature and pressure, pure water is colorless, odorless, and tasteless. \n\n### 3. \"The Universal Solvent\"\nWater has a unique chemical structure (it is \"polar,\" meaning the oxygen side has a slight negative charge and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge). Because of this, it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This property is vital because it allows water to carry chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes—whether through the bloodstream of an animal or the soil of a plant.\n\n### 4. Earth’s Lifeblood\n*   **Global Coverage:** About 71% of the Earth's surface is covered by water, mostly in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n*   **In Living Things:** Water makes up about 60% of the adult human body and roughly 70–90% of the weight of living organisms. Life cannot exist without it, which is why scientists look for water on other planets when searching for extraterrestrial life.\n\nIn short, water is a simple chemical combination that acts as the fundamental driving force of weather, geology, and biology on our planet."
